<?xml version="1.0"?>
<views>
  <view name="PRISM" rowsource="pwiz.Skyline.Model.Databinding.Entities.Transition" sublist="Results!*" uimode="proteomic">
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide.Protein" />
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide.Protein.Accession" />
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide.Protein.Gene" />
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide" />
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide.ModifiedSequence.UnimodIds" />
    <column name="Precursor.Charge" />
    <column name="Precursor.Mz" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.IsotopeDotProduct" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.DetectionQValue" />
    <column name="FragmentIon" />
    <column name="ProductCharge" />
    <column name="ProductMz" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.Area" />
    <!--
      Background is exported but nothing in PRISM reads it. It was added so the MS2 signal accounting
      (qc_report.ms2_signal) could total GROSS signal, since Skyline's Area is background-subtracted
      and an acquired total ion current is not. That accounting was removed in dotnet-v26.25.1; its
      replacement, prism ion-accounting, counts ions from the instrument files and needs no Skyline
      column. The column stays only because dropping it changes every export, and the merged_data
      columns that follow from it, for no gain. It is cheap - it is already on ChromInfo.

      Skyline's per-transition ion-count columns (LC Peak Transition Ion Count and its relatives) are
      NOT here, and the reason is cost. Skyline computes them per spectrum for every transition.
      Measured on the 6.5 GB FLARE document (46M transition rows, 93 replicates), a three-column
      report exported in 9.5 minutes; the same report plus five LC Peak ion-count columns was 13%
      done after 63 minutes, writing at 4.5 MB/min against the baseline's 122 - 27x slower per
      byte, projecting to 9-13 HOURS, single-threaded and holding 15 GB resident. The per-transition
      column ALONE is 29x slower per row (7.9M rows in 42 minutes; about 4 hours for the document),
      so the cost is Skyline reading each transition's chromatogram points, not the column count.
      Do not add one back: prism ion-accounting gets ion counts from the instrument files in
      single-digit minutes per file, and per-transition counts cannot be summed correctly anyway,
      because co-isolated peptides sharing a fragment extract the same detector counts.

      There used to be a second definition, Skyline-PRISM-Ions.skyr (view "PRISM-Ions"), which was this
      report plus LC Peak Transition Ion Count. It was removed together with qc_report.ms2_signal.
    -->
    <column name="Results!*.Value.Background" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.RetentionTime" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.StartTime" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.EndTime" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.Fwhm" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.ShapeCorrelation" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.Truncated" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.PeptideResult.ResultFile.Replicate.Name" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.PeptideResult.ResultFile.FileName" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.PeptideResult.ResultFile.TicArea" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.PeptideResult.ResultFile.AcquiredTime" />
  </view>
</views>